# Simplify before splitting

A long file is not automatically a split. First ask whether the code
can be **reduced** without changing behavior or violating other rules:
duplication, unused paths, extra curry/wrappers, work the caller already
does.

Split into another file **only** when a responsibility has clear bounds
and a reason to change on its own. Splitting to “organize” or to hide
typing noise is the same smell as a zero-responsibility function.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — extract a file that only re-exports the same walk
// ❌ BAD — keep a fallback UI the host never uses “just in case”

// ✅ GOOD — delete the unused path; keep the recursive walk together
// ✅ GOOD — a real second job (e.g. overlay submit) lives in its own file
```

Related: `no-zero-responsibility.mdc`.
